<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#south-picene-language">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Picene language</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as South Picene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">South Picene is an extinct Sabellic language of the Italic family, known from about two dozen inscriptions of the 6th to 4th centuries BCE found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was written in a distinctive alphabet adapted from Etruscan, and its texts, mostly on funerary stelae, were deciphered only in the late 20th century. The inscription on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warrior-of-capestrano">Warrior of Capestrano</a> is a famous example.</p></div>
